+++ b/Docs/MASINISSA_AUTORUN_FIXES.md
@@ -0,0 +1,155 @@
+# Autorun as Masinissa: endless turn, turn time, settlers, economy
+
+Reported on a Marathon game played on autorun as Masinissa, with the save
+`Masinissa BC-0410.CivBeyondSwordSave` (410 BC, game turn 269):
+
+1. Landing fix works.
+2. Settler mechanics still unripe.
+3. AI still not valuing key buildings and technologies properly.
+4. "Waiting for other players" at end of turn never ends, even after reloading.
+5. Worse time complexity.
+
+Every finding below comes from loading that save into the real DLL built from
+this source and letting all players (Masinissa on autoplay) play on: a headless
+harness runs `CvGameCoreDLL.dll` under Wine with the mod's XML, reads the save
+through the DLL's own `read()` functions and records every turn
+(GertaBool/RoM, `toolchain/harness`). The game executable was not run.
+
+## 4. The turn that never ends
+
+At turn 269 the unfixed DLL calls `CvGame::update` forever: one AI group is
+updated, reports that it acted, and the next slice starts with the same group.
+
+The group is a single Javelineer with the worker-style job of improving a plot.
+Its path ends on a tile held by an enemy unit. The path finder accepts that step
+(a combat unit could take it by attacking), but `groupMove` without
+`MOVE_THROUGH_ENEMY` moves each unit that cannot enter the tile into a new
+group. A lone unit thus leaves its group, the old group dies, the new group has
+moves left and gets the same order again. No moves are ever spent, so the turn
+cannot end. Reloading recreates the same state.
+
+- `CvSelectionGroup::groupPathTo` ends the mission when no unit of the group
+  can take the next step as the move it was asked for (the same test
+  `groupMove` applies).
+- `AI_improveLocalPlot` and `AI_improveBonus` skip plots with visible enemy
+  units, so such a job is not chosen in the first place.
+- Watchdog: when one unit loses its group more than 20 times in one turn while
+  it can still move, its moves end (and a debug assert fires).
+
+With the fix the save plays on (turns 269 to 299 checked).
+`Tools/test_turn_end_guard.py` compiles `groupPathTo` and the watchdog from
+this source with the engine's group-splitting rule.
+
+## 5. Turn time
+
+On the save, turns 270 to 279 took 9.6 s each with the previous DLLs (d8cedd5
+through patch 0011; they did not differ) and take 2.6 s now (harness timings,
+same machine). The profile showed most of the time in path searches. Changes,
+in order of effect:
+
+- `pathValid` looks at the parent plot only, except for one sea-corner test,
+  and the A* asks once for every neighbour of the plot it expands. The parent
+  part is now kept for the plot being expanded and dropped whenever a search
+  starts. Same answers: the game state after ten turns is byte-identical.
+- `AI_explore` used to run a path search to every candidate plot on the map.
+  Candidates are now searched best bound first, and the search stops once no
+  remaining candidate can win (the bound uses the largest possible random
+  draw and the fewest turns the path could take).
+- `AI_exploreRange` skips plots that cannot be reached within the range, using
+  a lower bound on path turns from the group's moves and the map's cheapest
+  route. Exact: same game state.
+- `CvUnit::upgradeAvailable` searched the upgrade tree recursively on every
+  call; the reachable classes are now computed once per civilization and unit.
+  `AI_upgrade` returns early when the unit cannot upgrade now, scans only
+  classes in the unit's upgrade tree, and takes the most valuable upgrade
+  (it used to pick a random one among those better than the unit).
+- `CvPlayer::canTradeItem(..., bTestDenial)` checks whether the item is
+  tradeable before computing the denial (the denial is the expensive part).
+- `RoMGameUtils.py`: the pillage and capture gold callbacks no longer loop over
+  every building of the player to find Himeji Castle.
+
+## 2. Settlers
+
+At 410 BC 18 settlers existed and 15 had no destination; one had been aboard
+a galley for 64 turns. For each of them the harness evaluated every test `AI_found` applies:
+
+- **Unreachable sites.** Ivan's four settlers (11 to 34 turns old) had one
+  candidate, behind other civilizations' borders: no `MOVE_SAFE_TERRITORY`
+  path and no boat. Willem's settlers sat in a city walled in by foreign
+  borders; the sites were reachable only from his other cities. The cities
+  kept training settlers for such sites because the site count did not ask
+  whether a settler could get there, and since the settlement rework a settler
+  without a site was never disbanded.
+- **No escort.** Lone settlers wait in a city for an escort. Defenders join
+  them only when no danger is near, so in a city near a war none ever came.
+- **Galley at sea without escort.** Joao's galley had carried a settler for 64
+  turns: with no escort aboard it skipped every turn at sea, where none can
+  board.
+- **Islands.** Settlers whose sites need a boat waited for one nobody built.
+
+Changes:
+
+- `CvPlayerAI::AI_updateSettleReach` (with the found values, once per update):
+  land regions grow from each of our cities through revealed land that is ours
+  or unclaimed, the plots a `MOVE_SAFE_TERRITORY` path may cross; sea regions
+  grow from our ports over water that a transport we have or can train may
+  enter (its terrain and feature limits and the team's techs). A party is
+  delivered by land within its region, or by sea from a port of its region to
+  a coastal site next to that water. A party outside these regions (at sea,
+  landed abroad) is not limited.
+- The production portfolio and the city's settler counts only count sites a
+  settler trained there can be delivered to. `AI_found` and the settler
+  transport skip the others (also saving their failing path searches).
+- A settler older than max(20, 10 turns scaled by the train percent) with no
+  deliverable site is disbanded (BTS did this after 20 turns), checked before
+  the danger rules so a threatened city cannot keep it forever.
+- A lone settler in our city takes a spare defender along: one above the
+  city's minimum garrison, alone and idle or guarding this city.
+- A settler transport at sea without an escort sails to a port.
+- A city with a waiting settler whose deliverable sites all need a boat
+  builds a settler transport when none serves that water.
+
+`Tools/test_settle_reach.py` compiles the region code with a grid map: a city
+behind foreign borders, sea delivery with and without a trainable transport,
+and terrain the transport may only enter with a tech.
+
+## 3. Buildings and technologies
+
+Two defects explain most of what the harness shows at 410 BC:
+
+- **The Spearman pump.** The minimal-garrison rule counts only units with the
+  `UNITAI_CITY_DEFENSE` role. A unit qualifies through its XML or a city
+  defence bonus. Spearmen have neither, and 9 of the 18 civilizations had
+  neither Archery nor Bronze Working. A city that cannot train such a unit
+  failed the garrison test at every production decision, fell back to an
+  attack-role unit (a Spearman) that never counted, and built the next one:
+  54 of about 150 production choices in five turns were Spearmen, and 12
+  civilizations had no city-defence unit at all. BBAI handled this for Warriors in the first era only. A
+  city that cannot train any city-defence unit now counts all its land
+  defenders, in every era.
+- **Gold hoarding on slow speeds.** `AI_goldTarget` adds half the elapsed turns
+  and then scales by the speed's price multiplier, but a slow speed already
+  has more turns for the same stretch of history: on Marathon the term was
+  about 8 times its Normal size, and the commerce cap used the research
+  percent (600) as turns of liquidity. Targets of 640 to 1270 gold against
+  50 to 420 commerce per turn held several AIs at 5 to 25 percent research
+  (Charles V: 473 gold banked, 15 percent research). Elapsed turns now count at
+  the standard speed's pace and the cap uses the calendar ratio (2.1 turns on
+  Marathon). Normal speed is unchanged. `Tools/test_gold_target_speed.py`
+  checks both.
+
+Over 30 turns from the save, compared with the same source without these two
+changes: research +21 percent (cumulative beakers), technologies gained 100
+instead of 79, buildings completed 347 instead of 238, 857 instead of 915
+military units at the end.
+
+Not changed: the technology values themselves looked sensible (for example
+Rome ranked Writing, Seafaring and Bronze Working highest). The Brewery has the
+highest building value in every city that can build it (about 1700, from the
+free Alcohol resource counted for every city), but hardly any are built, so it
+does not drive production.
+
+## Compatibility
+
+No saved data changed; existing saves load. New members are transient and
+rebuilt after loading. The DLL exports the same 2451 symbols.
